---
/**
 * The hero: a full-bleed, scroll-scrubbed film of the entire pipeline.
 *
 * The camera travels a scene four times wider than the viewport — feed → ingest → match →
 * classify → post → report — on ONE light palette throughout, so the film hands off into the
 * ordinary light content below with no seam and no dark act. Scroll IS the transport: the payment
 * moves because you moved.
 *
 * WHY IT IS BUILT THIS WAY, in order of what would break first if ignored:
 *
 * 1. THE TALL SCROLL HEIGHT IS SET BY JS, NEVER BY CSS. Without the script the section is exactly
 *    one viewport tall and shows act one — a complete, readable hero. Had the 460vh lived in the
 *    stylesheet, a failed script would leave four blank screens to scroll past, which is the
 *    ADR 0012 §5 failure in its most expensive form.
 * 2. COLOUR IS SIX CSS VARIABLES, READ FROM THE STYLESHEET, NEVER TWEENED. Every mark and every
 *    word of copy reads them from `[data-stage]` (global.css) — the same values whether the film
 *    is running, static, or not yet hydrated — so there is nothing for JS to get right or wrong
 *    about colour, and no seam between "hero" and the page.
 * 3. THE HEADER DOES NOT INVERT. With no dark act there is nothing for it to invert against — it
 *    keeps its ordinary light treatment for the whole scroll.
 * 4. NO PINNING BELOW 900px OR UNDER REDUCED MOTION. Both fall back to the same static hero as
 *    the no-JS case, rather than to a degraded version of the film.
 */
import { url } from '@/lib/url'
import CtaButton from '@/components/CtaButton.astro'

/**
 * Act one's feed, drawn as a bank statement rather than a list of strings — the design is doing
 * work here, not decorating: a statement is the artefact the reader currently reconciles by hand,
 * so it is what they recognise.
 *
 * Two rows are ACME at $48,500.00. That repetition is the product in one glance — the duplicate is
 * already sitting in the raw feed, and finding it is the tedious part.
 *
 * `tick` marks the rows that become payments on the rail, so the eye can follow one item from
 * statement line to posted credit.
 */
const FEED = [
  { desc: 'WIRE IN NORTHWIND TRADING INV-1042', amt: '$48,500.00', tick: true },
  { desc: 'ACH CREDIT ACME ENTERPRISES INV1051', amt: '$12,000.00', tick: true },
  { desc: 'CHECK DEPOSIT 8842 NO MEMO', amt: '$9,150.00', tick: true },
  { desc: 'ACH CREDIT NORTHWIND INV1042 DUP?', amt: '$48,500.00', tick: false },
]

/**
 * The institutions the feed arrives from, drawn as glass orbs that drift under it.
 *
 * THE PATH GEOMETRY IS THE BANKS' REAL MARKS, from Simple Icons (simpleicons.org), whose icon set
 * is released CC0-1.0 — public domain, no attribution required for the path data. The TRADEMARKS
 * remain the banks' own; they appear here to say what Verity connects to, which is the nominative
 * use `/terms` already reserves. Brand colours are Simple Icons' documented hex for each.
 *
 * VENDORED, NOT HOTLINKED. Inlined below rather than fetched from a CDN at runtime, because
 * `/security` and `/privacy` both claim this site makes no third-party request while you read it.
 * A logo pulled from jsdelivr on load would quietly make both pages false.
 *
 * Six US institutions, which is what Simple Icons actually carries under a CC0 licence — Citi,
 * Capital One, PNC and Truist have no mark in the set, so they are absent for availability
 * reasons rather than editorial ones.
 *
 * Plaid is deliberately NOT an orb. The orbs are institutions the money sits at; Plaid is the pipe
 * it arrives through, and is named in the heading instead.
 *
 * POSITIONS HERE ARE THE SERVER-RENDERED LAYOUT and are asserted to be in-bounds and
 * non-overlapping, because this is exactly what ships when the physics never runs.
 */
